<p class="guide-lead">
  A percentage describes a relationship: how many members of a group satisfy a condition, out of everyone counted in that group.
</p>
<p>
  Consider the question, “What percentage of adults have ever entered a body of salt water?” Before looking for a number, we need to say what would count as an answer.
</p>
<section id="percentage-definition" class="guide-definition" aria-labelledby="definition-title">
  <h2 id="definition-title">The shape of a percentage</h2>
  <p class="guide-sentence">
    The share of <strong>a population</strong>
    who satisfy <strong>a condition</strong>
    at <strong>a stated time</strong>.
  </p>
  <dl>
    <dt>Population</dt><dd>
      Who is included? For this example: people aged 18 or older, alive on a specified date.
    </dd>
    <dt>Condition</dt><dd>
      What must be true of them? They had entered a naturally occurring body of salt water by that date, under a stated meaning of “entered.”
    </dd>
    <dt>Time</dt><dd>
      When does the statement apply? Both the adult population and each person’s experience can change.
    </dd>
    <dt>Proportion</dt><dd>
      The number satisfying the condition divided by the number in the population. Multiply by 100 to express it as a percentage.
    </dd>
  </dl>
</section>
<h2 id="percentage-statements">From an answer to a statement</h2>
<p>
  A question asks for information. An option such as “Yes” gets its meaning from the question. “Has entered salt water” names the condition; applying it to a particular person and date forms a proposition that can be true or false.
</p>
<p>
  For a frequency question, “Sometimes” needs the same care. “Sometimes cooks at home” is more informative, but still depends on the reference period and what “sometimes” means in that question. A full sentence must preserve the meaning of the original answer.
</p>

<h2 id="percentage-evidence">The quantity and the evidence</h2>
<p>
  The <strong>target proportion</strong>
  is the quantity we want to learn about. The <strong>observed proportion</strong>
  is calculated from the evidence we actually have. An <strong>estimate</strong>
  uses evidence and a method to infer a target quantity.
</p>
<p>
  In a hypothetical survey, if 60 of 100 eligible respondents answer “Yes,” the observed share is 60%. Whether that estimates the share of all adults depends on who was included, who answered, and how well the question measured the experience.
</p>
<p>
  A result statement should retain the population, time, source, method, and denominator that give the number its meaning. Missing answers, results withheld for privacy, and a population with no members must not appear as a measured zero.
</p>
<h2 id="percentage-populations">Populations can be defined through other answers</h2>
<p>
  Someone can answer a question about their age group and a question about an experience. Their answers can then contribute to an observed share among respondents in that age group who also answered the experience question.
</p>
<p>
  People with no age-group answer have unknown qualification. They do not become members of the other groups. An age calculated from a birthday also needs an evaluation date; it is a different observation from selecting an age-group option.
</p>
<p>
  These intersections describe recorded respondents. They do not verify identities, establish representative sampling, or show that age caused a difference. Multiple-selection questions can create overlapping groups and shares that add to more than 100%.
</p>
